    planner tail scenarios: 27 of 500
    agents' tail scenarios: min 25, max 26  -> the agents do not agree on where the tail is
    max_k |gamma_k - gamma| = 0.012000   (Eq. 94 requires 0)

==============================================================================
E2  Risk incidence by generation technology
==============================================================================
  At equilibrium the insurance price is q = rho_tilde, so the premium an
  agent pays in excess of its expected payout is sum_w (q_w - rho_w) r_w.
  Under the corrected Eq. (111)-(112), r_k = L_k - l/N - delta_k, and that
  splits exactly into
      OWN RISK   pi_k := sum_w (q_w - rho_w) L_kw     (agent-specific)
      POOL SHARE       -(1/N) sum_w (q_w - rho_w) l_w (identical for all)
  The delta_k drop out of both. pi_k is the economically meaningful term:
  the equilibrium price of the tail risk that agent k's OWN profit stream
  brings to the market. The pool share is a flat rebate that the
  equal-share rule hands every agent regardless of contribution.

  Eq.(95) clearing check: max_w |sum_k r_k(w)| = 1.118e-08

  flat pool share handed to every agent: -61,861.42 $/h

  technology                 n   own risk pi_k $/h   $/MW capacity   equal-share net
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  solar                      6            3,464.11           1.103       -367,704.43
  wind                       6           -2,670.89          -0.866       -373,839.44
  BIT (Bituminous Coal)      1                0.00           0.000        -61,861.42
  NG (Natural Gas)           3               28.96           0.002       -185,555.31
  NUC (Nuclear)              2               -0.00          -0.000       -123,722.85
  LOAD                       1        1,174,544.88          41.790      1,112,683.46
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  RENEWABLE (solar+wind)                    793.23           0.127
  THERMAL                                    28.96           0.001
  LOAD                                1,174,544.88

  share of total |tail-risk premium|  (renewable share 14.5%):
    renewables   0.1%    thermal   0.0%    load  99.3%
  The load is the residual risk bearer: pi_LOAD > 0 means its loss spikes
  exactly in the scenarios the market prices most heavily, so it is the
  natural BUYER of cover. Renewables are mildly negative (they earn more
  in scarcity when they do produce), so they SELL cover.
  pi is EXACTLY zero for coal and nuclear, and that is a property of the
  dataset, not of the theory: thermal capacity in thermal_gens.csv is
  constant across scenarios, so an infra-marginal unit has a constant L
  and sum_w (q_w - rho_w) L = L * 0 = 0. Modelling forced outages would
  give thermal genuine tail exposure; we flag this rather than hide it.
  NOTE the equal-share column is nearly identical across generators: the
  flat pool share swamps the signal. That is a property of the Eq. (111)
  rule, not of the market, and is worth saying in the paper -- a
  contribution-weighted delta_k would allocate the rebate sensibly.

==============================================================================
done in 165.6s
==============================================================================

